/*****************************************************************************
    jitPrintT -- DOES A JITTED PRINT FIRE AT RUN TIME?

    Work item 3's proof. Until 2026-08-04 a jitted print fired AT COMPILE TIME:
    once, reporting compile-time state. That is worse than not printing, because
    it appears to work -- and it breaks the effect-free-emit law the ladder's
    whole compile-once-fire-twice argument already assumed.

    THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE PAIR OF FIRES, and it separates three worlds:
      emit-time print   -> ONE line, value 7, and NOTHING on the refire
      run-time, folded  -> TWO lines, both 7
      run-time, correct -> TWO lines, 7 then 9        <- the claim

    So the count of lines and their values answer different questions and both
    are asserted. The value moves AFTER emission, which is the same run-time
    proof every other rung makes, aimed at an effect instead of at a return.

    ⚠⚠ STATUS 2026-08-05: THE BARE-READ PRIMITIVE WORKS; A MULTI-PART EXPRESSION
    IN A PRINT DOES NOT. Read this before assuming the fixture is broken.

        pBare  `print pVal;`               fire 1 = 41, fire 2 = 58   ✅ CORRECT
        pAct   `print "P value =" pVal:;`  garbage on both fires      ✗ OPEN

    pBare is the win and it is a real one: a bare operand now MATERIALIZES, and
    the value tracks an input changed AFTER emission, so it is computed at run
    time. That is the first jitted print to carry a correct value.

    pAct is a separate, named gap and NOT the same bug. Its PrintXP item carries a
    multi-part expression list (`xl`), which emits SOMETHING -- the degrade
    counter stays 0, so the refusal in jitPrintItem does not fire -- but the
    something is not the count appendGroupValue expects. appendGroupValue's phase
    scope is i32 counts; a string entry is named there as future work. The
    refusal catches "emitted nothing" and does NOT catch "emitted the wrong
    kind", which is the next thing to fix and is written here so it is not
    rediscovered.

    ⚠ THE GARBAGE GOT WORSE AS THE EMITTERS GOT BETTER, and that is the lesson
    worth keeping: before the primitive existed this printed 0 -- wrong, but
    stable. Once real values flowed it printed 80329152, a STALE read wearing the
    shape of data. jitPrintArm now clears the in-flight value per item so a null
    means "this item emitted nothing" rather than "nobody ever emitted", which is
    what lets jitPrintItem refuse instead of substitute. The remaining garbage is
    past that guard, not through a hole in it.

    ⚠ HISTORICAL, 2026-08-04: THE LAW IS SATISFIED, THE CONTENT WAS NOT.

    WHAT IS CERTIFIED -- the print now fires ONCE PER FIRE, at RUN time. Two
    lines for two fires, where the old behaviour produced ONE, at compile time,
    and nothing on the refire. That is the effect-free-emit law's actual
    requirement and it holds. The bracket emits correctly too; the IR reads

        %printBuf  = call jitPrintBegin(stmt)
        %printVal  = call appendGroupValue(i32 0, null, %printBuf)
        %printTok  = call appendGroup(<token>, null, %printBuf)
        %printSink = call opPrint(stmt, %printBuf)

    WHAT IS NOT -- the per-item VALUES. Two defects, both visible above:
      1. `%printVal` is handed a CONSTANT i32 0, not an SSA value: gJitResult is
         null when jitPrintItem runs, so the expression emitters did not leave a
         value for that item. Printing `0` is that constant, faithfully.
      2. TWO items were walked where the statement has three parts. The item
         count does not match a plain reading of `print "P value =" pVal:;`, so
         the PrintXP shape is not what the walk assumes -- and that is a question
         about the shape, to be measured, not guessed at.

    ⚠ NOT WIRED INTO THE LADDER, deliberately. A green row here would claim the
    values work. The fixture is committed as the reproduction and the record.

    ⚠ THE SHORTCUT AND THE LITERAL ARE IN THE STATEMENT ON PURPOSE. `print "P
    value =" pVal:;` carries a literal, an expression and the `:` newline
    shortcut, so it exercises all three arms of the seam -- appendGroupValue
    passing a token through untouched, stamping a value on a carrier, and the
    chain's own shortcut switch -- rather than only the arm that is new.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitPrintT);
define
    pVal;
    pAct code={
        print "P value =" pVal:;
        };
    /*  THE DISCRIMINATOR, added 2026-08-05 after the R3 printout: a print whose
        expression is a BARE NUMERIC FIELD, no literal, no concatenation. If this
        one carries its value and the concatenating one does not, the plumbing is
        right and the gap is the STRING EXPRESSION -- which is phase scope, not a
        print bug. If neither carries a value, the plumbing is wrong.  */
    pBare code={
        print pVal;
        };
    ;

pVal = 7;
print "-- FIRE 1 (compiles; the line below must come from RUN time) --":;
testing(pAct);

pVal = 9;
print "-- FIRE 2 (no recompile; must print again, with 9) --":;
jitRefire();

print "-- BARE NUMERIC EXPRESSION (the discriminator) --":;
pVal = 41;
testing(pBare);
pVal = 58;
print "-- bare, fire 2 (want 58) --":;
jitRefire();

print "JP2 SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
